TWO TESTS DEMANDED A MECHANISM THE PROJECT RETIRED, AND FAILED ON CLEAN DEV.

  test_smart_test_slice_robustness.py::test_baseline_detection_is_failover_aware
  test_smart_test_coverage_gate.py::test_baseline_detection_still_failover_aware

Both asserted scripts/smart-test CONTAINS the string CI_FAILOVER_ACTIVE.
WI-hajif step 1 (e7e43d678d) retired the self-hosted-Forgejo failover, updated
get_baseline, and added tests/test_ci_failover_retired.py -- a recurrence gate
that FORBIDS that string. The suite held a test asserting X and a test
asserting not-X, each correct by its own lights, the gate green and the other
two red. Verified: `git show dev:scripts/smart-test | grep -c CI_FAILOVER_ACTIVE`
-> 0.

WHY CI NEVER NOTICED. Nothing but scripts/smart-test maps to those two files,
so the per-PR selection never picks them up -- L48 at the level of a retirement
sweep rather than a single test. They surfaced only because
check-package-coverage was run for an unrelated YAML change.

REWRITTEN, NOT DELETED. The property they protected is still real -- a baseline
anchored on something stale inflates the changed set and forces a whole-repo
slice -- and only the mechanism moved. Both now assert the post-retirement
contract: a `git merge-base HEAD` against origin/dev, the sole authoritative
remote. Deleting them would have dropped the anchoring assertion entirely.

THE GATE NOW SCANS THE TEST TREES, third instance of its own header lesson. Its
comment already records that the first draft scanned only scripts/ and
.githooks/ and went green while two other trees still carried the machinery,
quoting L23. It then scanned five SOURCE trees and no test tree.

RESIDUE DECLARED AS DATA RATHER THAN SUPPRESSED. Widening surfaced six further
files of a DIFFERENT kind: they defensively scrub the retired forge's
credential env vars, or stub apply_failover_overrides to a no-op in a fixture.
Those assert nothing and cannot resurrect anything -- untidy, not wrong. They
are listed in KNOWN_VESTIGIAL_RESIDUE, which MAY ONLY SHRINK: a companion test
fails if an entry stops referencing the tokens, so a cleanup cannot leave the
list stale, and a genuinely new offender is not covered by it. Deliberately not
cleaned here -- bundling a speculative rewrite of forge-test fixtures into a
fix for two red tests risks breaking harnesses to satisfy a regex.

THE NON-VACUITY FLOOR WAS STRENGTHENED AFTER ITS OWN MUTATION TEST FOUND A HOLE
IN THIS CHANGE. It asserted the scan reaches .agent, packages and scripts -- but
`packages` is satisfied by the src tree alone, so dropping
packages/hypergumbo-core/tests from SCANNED_DIRS left it green: exactly how the
original gate missed the test trees. It now asserts both test trees. Mutation
tested four ways: drop an offender from the residue list -> main gate fails;
add a cleaned entry -> ratchet fails; name a missing file -> ratchet fails;
drop either test tree -> floor fails.

ALSO: check-package-coverage reported TEST FAILURES as COVERAGE GAPS. The run
that exposed all of the above printed "Coverage gaps for core:" followed by an
EMPTY list, then "Some packages have coverage gaps when tested in isolation"
and two suggested fixes -- move the test to the other package, stop using
subprocess -- while its own log said "TOTAL 29770 0 100%" and "Required test
coverage of 100% reached". A reader trusting the banner would hunt a
test-placement problem that does not exist. Both report sites now route through
one report_failure helper that names the failing test ids when tests failed and
reserves the coverage-gap advice for an actual shortfall. Verified by replaying
the original log, which now names both failing tests.

